ME/CFS Post-Viral EBV Onset
Complex Fatigue Entities
Insidious fatigue following Glandular Fever

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-031
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe pharyngitis, profound physical collapse, and inability to maintain oral hydration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, severe relapse of physical symptoms including extreme lethargy, swollen neck glands, and inability to swallow fluids safely. Immediate in-home evaluation was required to rule out a peritonsillar abscess or acute airway compromise, and to manage a severe post-exertional crash that could not be safely delayed until morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My throat is burning, my neck glands are huge, and I am too weak to even lift my head off the pillow.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute exacerbation of ME/CFS triggered by a minor psychological stressor yesterday. The condition began insidiously months ago following acute Epstein-Barr Virus Glandular Fever. The current flare evolved over 24 hours, starting with a recurrent, severe sore throat, followed by marked swelling of the cervical lymph nodes, and culminating in severe Post-Exertional Malaise (PEM). The patient reports feeling utterly devoid of cellular energy, unable to chew food.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound. Requires assistance to sit up or drink.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dehydration due to painful swallowing; highly dependent on parent for basic care tonight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Pale, exhausted, speaking in a faint whisper to conserve energy.
Physical Exam: Oropharynx reveals mild bilateral tonsillar erythema without exudate; no peritonsillar swelling or uvular deviation. Airway is patent. Marked, tender anterior and posterior cervical lymphadenopathy present bilaterally. No signs of respiratory distress. Neurological and abdominal exams are benign. Findings are consistent with insidious fatigue following Glandular Fever with active localized immune reactivity.
Environmental Observations: Dimmed room lighting; patient strictly avoiding sensory input.
Medication Reconciliation: No active prescriptions. Uses over-the-counter lozenges with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: ME/CFS Post-Viral EBV Onset - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Peritonsillar Abscess (Quinsy) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of unilateral tonsillar swelling, no uvular deviation, and normal voice (not "hot potato" voice).

Acute Streptococcal Pharyngitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of high-grade fever, absence of purulent tonsillar exudate, and chronicity of similar post-exertional flares.

Acute HIV Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Established prior history of EBV infection driving identical, recurrent crashes, though initial workup requires serological confirmation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's severe sore throat, prominent lymphadenopathy, and inability to maintain fluid intake required an urgent face-to-face assessment to physically exclude a life-threatening peritonsillar abscess or acute airway obstruction. Having confirmed an anatomically secure airway and stable hemodynamics, it was determined the patient is suffering from a severe immune-mediated post-exertional crash. Emergency transport would have catastrophically worsened the metabolic exhaustion; therefore, aggressive rest and symptomatic pharmacological management were instituted in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of oral Meloxicam 7.5mg daily for symptomatic relief of lymph node and pharyngeal inflammation. Advised strict sensory rest. Targeted intervention strategy includes LDN, Vitamin C, Zinc, and Vitamin D supplementation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By utilizing a numbing throat spray exactly 5 minutes before attempting to drink, you will actively override the pain response, directly allowing you to swallow electrolyte fluids and rewarding your body with the critical hydration needed to recover from this crash.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Humidifier for the bedroom to soothe the pharynx.
Follow-up: Telehealth GP review in 7 days to monitor ME/CFS baseline and coordinate metabolic support.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if the patient develops drooling, inability to swallow saliva, stridor (noisy breathing), or unrousable lethargy.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Post-exertional crashes mimicking the initial viral infection are hallmarks of this condition; no acute antibiotics are indicated for this functional immune flare.
Clinical Course: The acute lymph node pain and throat burning will slowly recede over the next 48-72 hours. Returning to a functional baseline will require strict pacing and prolonged rest.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Parent must call 000 immediately if the patient starts drooling, struggles to breathe, or if their voice drastically changes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is misfiring, recreating the symptoms of your original glandular fever without an active virus present; the anti-inflammatory medication will calm the swollen glands so you can drink safely.
Actionable Steps: Remain absolutely flat in bed tomorrow and avoid all electronic screens; aggressive rest immediately stops further cellular energy drain, positively reinforcing a faster exit from the crash phase.

